# ── Terminal app: LLM backend ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# OPTIONAL. Without a key the Terminal still works: unrecognized commands get an
# in-character canned reply instead of a real model response. Add a key to make
# the assistant live.
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in your key. .env.local is gitignored —
# the key is read server-side only, in app/api/terminal/route.ts, and never
# reaches the browser.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Model used by the terminal assistant. Defaults to claude-haiku-4-5-20251001,
# which is fast and cheap — the right trade for one-line terminal replies.
# TERMINAL_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001

# ── WhisperFlow app: dictation backend ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# OPTIONAL, exactly like the key above. Without it WhisperFlow starts in DEMO
# MODE: it replays scripted transcripts instead of listening, so the app is
# fully explorable with no key, no microphone and no internet.
#
# With a key, WhisperFlow records from the microphone and transcribes for real.
# The audio is POSTed to /api/whisper on THIS server, and the server calls
# OpenAI — the browser never talks to openai.com directly. That is what keeps
# the site's "no cross-origin requests from the page" guarantee intact.
#
# Read server-side only, in app/api/whisper/route.ts. There is deliberately no
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ variant: a public key would be readable by every visitor.
#
# ── BEFORE YOU DEPLOY WITH A KEY, READ THIS ─────────────────────────────────
#
# /api/whisper has NO AUTHENTICATION. Anyone who can reach the deployed site can
# post audio to it and spend this key's credits — no account, no sign-in, no
# referer check. That is a deliberate trade for a portfolio demo rather than an
# oversight, but it is only a safe one if you bound the spend.
#
# What the route does bound: one take is capped at 60 seconds (enforced as
# ~2.8 MB of upload, since a server cannot measure duration without decoding),
# 8 transcriptions per IP per minute, and 60 per minute across everyone. Those
# limits live in this server's memory: they reset on every redeploy, they do not
# coordinate across serverless instances, and the IP they key on comes from
# x-forwarded-for, which a caller can set to anything unless a proxy you trust
# is overwriting it.
#
# So they slow abuse down; they cannot stop it. The mitigation that actually
# holds is on OpenAI's side:
#
#   1. Set a hard monthly spend limit on the project this key belongs to
#      (platform.openai.com → Settings → Limits). Do this first.
#   2. Use a project-scoped key that can reach nothing but the audio endpoints.
#   3. Leave this blank on any deployment you are not watching. WhisperFlow is
#      fully explorable in demo mode with no key at all — that is the whole
#      point of demo mode.
#
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Transcription model. Defaults to whisper-1, which every OpenAI account has.
# WHISPER_MODEL=whisper-1
